func TestReverse(t *testing.T) {
	tests := []struct{ in, want string }{
		{"", ""},
		{"a", "a"},
		{"hello", "olleh"},
		{"Hello, 世界", "界世 ,olleH"},
		{"héllo", "olléh"},
		{"🙂👍", "👍🙂"},
		{"a\xffb", "b\xffa"}, // invalid byte preserved
	}
	for _, tt := range tests {
		if got := Reverse(tt.in); got != tt.want {
			t.Errorf("Reverse(%q) = %q, want %q", tt.in, got, tt.want)
		}
	}
}
```

## Caveat: grapheme clusters

Both versions reverse code points. Some user-perceived characters are made of several code points, and those will be split apart:

- `"e\u0301"` (e + combining acute accent) reverses to the accent followed by `e`.
- Flag emoji like 🇺🇸 (two regional indicators) and ZWJ sequences like 👨‍👩‍👧 get scrambled.

If you need to reverse by visible character, segment the string into grapheme clusters first, using a library such as [`github.com/rivo/uniseg`](https://github.com/rivo/uniseg):

```go
import "github.com/rivo/uniseg"

func ReverseGraphemes(s string) string {
	var clusters []string
	g := uniseg.NewGraphemes(s)
	for g.Next() {
		clusters = append(clusters, g.Str())
	}
	buf := make([]byte, 0, len(s))
	for i := len(clusters) - 1; i >= 0; i-- {
		buf = append(buf, clusters[i]...)
	}
	return string(buf)
}
```

