# Worker Pool in Go

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
)

// WorkerPool spawns n goroutines (workers) that concurrently process
// jobs received from the jobs channel until it is closed.
func WorkerPool(jobs <-chan int, n int) {
	var wg sync.WaitGroup

	// Spawn n workers
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		wg.Add(1)
		go func(id int) {
			defer wg.Done()
			for job := range jobs { // exits when channel is closed
				processJob(id, job)
			}
		}(i)
	}

	wg.Wait() // wait for all workers to finish
}

// processJob handles a single job (replace with real logic).
func processJob(workerID, job int) {
	fmt.Printf("worker %d processing job %d\n", workerID, job)
}
```

## Example usage

```go
func main() {
	jobs := make(chan int, 10)

	// Start the pool
	done := make(chan struct{})
	go func() {
		WorkerPool(jobs, 3)
		close(done)
	}()

	// Send jobs
	for i := 1; i <= 9; i++ {
		jobs <- i
	}
	close(jobs) // closing signals workers to stop

	<-done // wait for pool to finish
	fmt.Println("all jobs done")
}
```

## Key points

- **`for job := range jobs`** — each worker loops until the `jobs` channel is closed, then exits cleanly.
- **`sync.WaitGroup`** — ensures `WorkerPool` doesn't return until all workers finish.
- **`jobs <-chan int`** — receive-only type prevents workers from sending jobs back into the channel.
- **Buffered channel (optional)** — buffering the jobs channel lets producers enqueue work without blocking if workers are busy.

If you need results back, a common extension is adding a `results chan<- int` parameter that workers write to.

