## Allocation-free-ish approach: decode forward, build backward

This avoids the intermediate `[]rune` slice by decoding runes from the front and appending them to a `[]byte` in reverse order.

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"unicode/utf8"
)

func Reverse(s string) string {
	if s == "" {
		return ""
	}

	// Find the byte offset of the last rune.
	_, size := utf8.DecodeLastRuneInString(s)

	buf := make([]byte, 0, len(s))
	for i := len(s); i > 0; {
		r, n := utf8.DecodeLastRuneInString(s[:i])
		buf = utf8.AppendRune(buf, r)
		i -= n
	}
	_ = size

	return string(buf)
}
```

Simpler variant of the same idea using `utf8.DecodeRuneInString` from the front:

```go
