}
```

## Important caveat: grapheme clusters

Reversing by code point will scramble text that uses combining marks or emoji sequences, because a single user-perceived character may consist of multiple runes.

```go
Reverse("é")        // "é"  (e + combining acute) — visually wrong
Reverse("👍🏽")      // "🏽👍" — modifier stays attached to the wrong base
```

If you need *grapheme*-correct reversal, use a library such as `github.com/rivo/uniseg`:

```go
import "github.com/rivo/uniseg"

func ReverseGraphemes(s string) string {
	gr := uniseg.NewGraphemes(s)
	var out []string
	for gr.Next() {
		out = append(out, gr.Str())
	}
	// reverse out
	for i, j := 0, len(out)-1; i < j; i, j = i+1, j-1 {
		out[i], out[j] = out[j], out[i]
	}
	res := ""
	for _, g := range out {
		res += g
	}
	return res
}
```

**Which to use:** the `[]rune` version is the idiomatic, easy-to-read choice for most cases. Use the grapheme-aware version if the input may contain combining characters, flags, or emoji with modifiers/skin tones.

## The trap

A naive byte reversal (`for i := len(s)-1; i >= 0; i--`) corrupts multi-byte UTF-8 characters, because a rune like `é` (U+00E9) is 2 bytes and `世` (U+4E16) is 3. You must reverse **code points**, not bytes.

## Recommended: decode backwards, byte by byte

This avoids allocating a `[]rune` slice and works directly on the string's bytes.

```go
package main

import (
	"strings"
	"unicode/utf8"
)

// Reverse returns s with its Unicode code points in reverse order.
// Invalid UTF-8 bytes are replaced with U+FFFD (the Unicode replacement character).
func Reverse(s string) string {
	if s == "" {
		return ""
	}

	var b strings.Builder
	b.Grow(len(s))

	for len(s) > 0 {
		r, size := utf8.DecodeLastRuneInString(s)
		b.WriteRune(r)
		s = s[:len(s)-size]
	}

	return b.String()
}
```

`utf8.DecodeLastRuneInString` walks backwards from the end of the string, finding the start of the last rune and returning it along with its encoded size. Slicing `s[:len(s)-size]` then removes that rune, so the loop peels one code point off the right at a time.

## Simpler alternative: convert to `[]rune`

