	for i, j := 0, len(runes)-1; i < j; i, j = i+1, j-1 {
		runes[i], runes[j] = runes[j], runes[i]
	}
	return string(runes)
}
```

This is the version most Go tutorials show. It's correct and readable, but it allocates a rune slice (4 bytes per code point) plus a second allocation for the result string.

