**How it works:**
- A `sync.WaitGroup` tracks the n worker goroutines.
- Each worker ranges over `jobs`, pulling one job at a time — this keeps memory usage low and lets the producer block when all workers are busy.
- `wg.Wait()` blocks the caller until every job has been processed and the `jobs` channel is closed.

**Example usage:**

```go
func main() {
	jobs := make(chan int, 100)
	for i := 1; i <= 10; i++ {
		jobs <- i
	}
	close(jobs) // signals workers to exit

	WorkerPool(jobs, 3) // 3 concurrent workers
}
```

**Notes:**
- The caller must close `jobs` to terminate the workers; otherwise `WorkerPool` blocks forever.
- If you need results, add a results channel parameter (e.g., `results chan<- int`) and send each processed value to it from the workers.

## Implementation

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
)

// WorkerPool starts n goroutines that consume jobs from the jobs channel and
// process them concurrently. It blocks until every job has been processed and
// all workers have exited.
//
// The caller is responsible for closing the jobs channel — workers exit when
// the channel is closed and drained.
func WorkerPool(jobs <-chan int, n int) {
	if n < 1 {
		n = 1
	}

	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	wg.Add(n)

	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		go func(id int) {
			defer wg.Done()
			for job := range jobs {
				process(id, job)
			}
		}(i)
	}

	wg.Wait()
}

// process is the unit of work each worker performs.
func process(workerID, job int) {
	fmt.Printf("worker %d processing job %d\n", workerID, job)
}
```

