Notes:
- Each goroutine ranges over `jobs`, so it keeps receiving work until the channel is closed.
- `sync.WaitGroup` ensures `WorkerPool` returns only after all workers exit.
- If `n` is less than or equal to zero, the function returns immediately without processing any jobs.
- Closing the `jobs` channel is the caller's responsibility to signal that no more work will be sent.

## Core implementation

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
)

// WorkerPool spawns n goroutines that consume jobs from the jobs channel and
// process them concurrently. It blocks until every job has been processed and
// all workers have exited.
//
// The caller must close(jobs) to signal "no more work" — workers exit when
// the channel is closed and fully drained.
func WorkerPool(jobs <-chan int, n int) {
	if n < 1 {
		n = 1
	}

	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	wg.Add(n)

	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		go func(workerID int) {
			defer wg.Done()
			for job := range jobs {
				process(workerID, job)
			}
		}(i)
	}

	wg.Wait()
}

// process stands in for whatever real work you need to perform.
func process(workerID, job int) {
	fmt.Printf("worker %d processed job %d\n", workerID, job)
}

func main() {
	jobs := make(chan int, 100) // buffered so the producer rarely blocks
	for i := 1; i <= 10; i++ {
		jobs <- i
	}
	close(jobs)

	WorkerPool(jobs, 3)
	fmt.Println("all done")
}
```

**How it works**

- `sync.WaitGroup` counts the workers; `wg.Wait()` blocks until the last one finishes, so no goroutines leak and the function returns only when the work is truly done.
- `for job := range jobs` is the idiomatic loop: it receives values until the channel is closed, then exits cleanly.
- The worker ID is passed as a parameter (Go 1.22+ makes the loop variable per-iteration, but passing it explicitly is clearer and works on older versions).

## More useful variants

Since the requested signature hardcodes the work, here's the generalized form — pass the job function in:

```go
func WorkerPoolFunc[T any](jobs <-chan T, n int, work func(workerID int, job T)) {
	if n < 1 {
		n = 1
	}

	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	wg.Add(n)

	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		go func(id int) {
			defer wg.Done()
			for job := range jobs {
				work(id, job)
			}
		}(i)
	}

	wg.Wait()
}
```

And a version that collects results, using a separate collector goroutine instead of a mutex:

```go
func WorkerPoolResults[T, R any](jobs <-chan T, n int, work func(T) R) []R {
	if n < 1 {
		n = 1
	}

	results := make(chan R, n)
	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	wg.Add(n)

	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		go func() {
			defer wg.Done()
			for job := range jobs {
				results <- work(job)
			}
		}()
	}

	go func() {
		wg.Wait()
		close(results)
	}()

	out := make([]R, 0)
	for r := range results {
		out = append(out, r)
	}
	return out
}
```

## Gotchas

- **You must close the channel.** If `jobs` is never closed, every worker stays blocked in `range` and `WorkerPool` never returns.
- **Only the producer closes `jobs`.** Closing it while another goroutine is still sending causes a panic.
- **Buffer the channel** (e.g. `make(chan int, 100)`) so producers don't block on every send.
- **Don't send on an unbuffered channel from inside a worker** to feed other workers — that deadlocks. Use a separate producer goroutine or a buffered channel.
- **Ordering is not preserved.** Results come back in completion order, not submission order. If you need ordering, index the results by job ID.
- `n` is a concurrency limit, not a queue size — with a buffered channel you can have many more jobs queued than workers.

## Basic implementation

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
)

// WorkerPool spawns n goroutines that consume jobs from the jobs channel.
// Each worker processes jobs until the channel is closed, at which point
// the worker exits. WorkerPool blocks until every worker has finished.
//
// The caller is responsible for closing the jobs channel to signal
// that no more work is coming.
func WorkerPool(jobs <-chan int, n int) {
	if n <= 0 {
		return
	}

	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	wg.Add(n)

	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		go func(id int) {
			defer wg.Done()
			for job := range jobs {
				process(id, job)
			}
		}(i)
	}

	wg.Wait()
}

func process(workerID, job int) {
	fmt.Printf("worker %d processed job %d\n", workerID, job)
}
```

## Usage

```go
func main() {
	jobs := make(chan int, 100)

	// A producer goroutine feeds the pool.
	go func() {
		defer close(jobs) // closing signals "no more jobs"
		for i := 1; i <= 10; i++ {
			jobs <- i
		}
	}()

	WorkerPool(jobs, 3) // blocks until all 10 jobs are done
	fmt.Println("all done")
}
```

## Variant that returns results

```go
// WorkerPoolResults is the same pattern, but each job's result is sent on
// the returned channel. The channel is closed automatically once all
// workers have exited, so callers can simply range over it.
func WorkerPoolResults(jobs <-chan int, n int) <-chan int {
	results := make(chan int, n)

	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	wg.Add(n)

	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		go func() {
			defer wg.Done()
			for job := range jobs {
				results <- job * 2 // your computation here
			}
		}()
	}

	go func() {
		wg.Wait()
		close(results)
	}()

	return results
}
```

```go
func main() {
	jobs := make(chan int, 10)
	go func() {
		defer close(jobs)
		for i := 1; i <= 5; i++ {
			jobs <- i
		}
	}()

	for r := range WorkerPoolResults(jobs, 2) {
		fmt.Println("result:", r)
	}
}
```

## Key points

- **`sync.WaitGroup`** tracks when all `n` goroutines have exited, so the function is safe to call synchronously.
- **`range jobs`** on a channel terminates when the channel is *closed*, which is how workers know to stop — the producer must `close(jobs)`.
- **Buffered `jobs` channel** (e.g. `make(chan int, 100)`) lets the producer run ahead instead of blocking on every send.
- **Results channel is buffered** with capacity `n` so workers never block on a send while a consumer is slow; the extra goroutine closes it after `wg.Wait()`.
- **Guard `n <= 0`** — otherwise you'd spawn zero workers and `wg.Wait()` would return immediately, silently dropping every job.

```go
